WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.760
Hi, I'm Majim Rahman, a graduate researcher from the department of

2
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.000
industrial engineering at the University of Pittsburgh where I worked with Dr.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.080
Lou and Dr. Rahimeian to recover solar cells and bicial panels.

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.680
Solar energy is one of the fastest growing renewable energy technologies.

5
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.439
However, solar panels do not last forever.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.599
Most modules have a lifetime of about 25 to 30 years. Then as solar deployment

7
00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:33.720
continues to increase, the amount of end of life PV waste is growing rapidly.

8
00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:38.000
Many end of life panels are landfilled and current recycling often recovers

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.480
only raw materials while destroying high value components.

10
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.440
So this leads to our key research question. Can intex silicon wafers be

11
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.440
recovered?

12
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.399
To answer this question, this work focuses on end of life bicial glass

13
00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:57.760
solar panels. These modules are important because they feature high

14
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.719
efficiency cells with glass layers on the front and the back.

15
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.000
The main goal is to separate the panel layers and recover valuable materials,

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.640
especially intact silicon wafers because they have higher use potential than

17
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.320
broken or fully processed silicon waste.

18
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.600
The key step is thermal elimination. And during wafer recovery, samples are

19
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.000
heated to 500° C to remove the EVA encapsulant and separate the panel

20
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.840
layers. The removal of clean EVA is essential for the recovery of induct.

21
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.280
After the encapsulant is removed, it becomes easier to separate the different

22
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.880
layers by allowing the recovery of materials including glass, aluminum,

23
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.840
copper, and notably silicon wafers. The experimental results revealed that

24
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.079
recovering intact wafers is feasible particularly when the samples have

25
00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:54.520
little or no pomech mechanical damage.

26
00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:59.040
Next, we evaluated the environmental impact using life cycle assessment. The

27
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.960
results indicate a significant reduction in the cumulative energy demand when

28
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.880
transitioning from laboratory to industrial scale. However, thermal

29
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.319
treatment remains the dominant energy contributor.

30
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.720
Finally, we evaluated the physibility of the strategy from an economic

31
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.720
standpoint. The analysis shows that recovered wafers can reduce model

32
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.480
manufacturing cost from 10.5 cents per watt peak to 9.2 cents per watt peak.

33
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.920
And when more than half of the wafers are fully recovered, the technique

34
00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.800
becomes commercially viable.

35
00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.280
So our study shows that end ofly bfacial solar panels can be transformed into

36
00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.920
valuable sources of recoverable glass and metals as well as reusable silicon

37
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.879
wafers supporting circular economic principles. So in summary, end of life

38
00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:52.879
solar panels do not have to become the waste. They have the potential to serve

39
00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:58.120
as valuable assets for future solar module development.

40
00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.959
Thank

